# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0

config MESHTASTIC_WATCHDOG
	bool "Task-level software watchdog (force-reset a wedged system)"
	default y
	select WATCHDOG
	select TASK_WDT
	help
	  Uses Zephyr's task_wdt (subsys/task_wdt) -- independent channels per
	  subsystem, each fed on its own schedule, with the SoC's hardware
	  watchdog timer (wdt0) wired as task_wdt's own fallback in case the
	  software layer itself wedges. Two channels today:
	    - "radio": fed by meshtastic_watchdog_checkin(), wired to
	      meshtastic_powermon_set() -- every LoRa TX/RX power-state
	      transition, plus the unconditional periodic AGC-reset timer, so it
	      ticks at least once every CONFIG_MESHTASTIC_AGC_RESET_INTERVAL_MS
	      regardless of mesh traffic.
	    - "sysworkq": fed by a periodic work item on the system workqueue,
	      independently of the radio -- catches a hang in that thread even
	      if the radio subsystem keeps ticking fine (a lot of Zephyr's own
	      net/mgmt glue also dispatches through the system workqueue, so
	      this indirectly covers more than its name alone suggests).

	  This exists for a specific, observed gap: every silent-hang outage seen
	  on the bench (a node goes dark with zero log activity, no assert, no
	  fatal-error trace, unresponsive to ping/telnet/SMP) left NOTHING for
	  CONFIG_DEBUG_COREDUMP/CONFIG_RESET_ON_FATAL_ERROR to catch, because
	  those only fire on a software fault Zephyr's own detection can see (a
	  CPU exception, an assert, a stack overflow). A watchdog doesn't care
	  *why* a channel stopped ticking, only that it did — so it catches the
	  class of hang nothing else here can, at the cost of turning "dead
	  until someone finds it and power-cycles it" into "auto-recovers
	  within one timeout window," and now tells you *which* channel went
	  quiet rather than just "something did."

if MESHTASTIC_WATCHDOG

config MESHTASTIC_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Per-channel watchdog timeout (ms)"
	default 180000
	range 60000 600000
	help
	  How long a channel can go unfed before its timeout callback fires
	  (writes the RTC crash breadcrumb, then reboots). Both channels share
	  this value. Set generously above the guaranteed once-per-AGC-reset-
	  interval tick (default 60 s) to leave real margin for scheduling
	  jitter, PM light-sleep wake latency, or a legitimately slow admin/OTA
	  operation — this is a last-resort backstop, not a tight liveness
	  probe.

	  Deliberately a different number from CONFIG_TASK_WDT_MIN_TIMEOUT
	  (which sizes the underlying *hardware* watchdog's own timeout, and is
	  capped at 10 s by its own Kconfig range) -- that's correct, not a
	  mismatch to fix: it gives the hardware fallback a much tighter trigger
	  than any one channel's own grace period, in case the software task_wdt
	  layer itself wedges.

config MESHTASTIC_WATCHDOG_FEED_INTERVAL_MS
	int "sysworkq heartbeat interval (ms)"
	default 30000
	range 5000 60000
	help
	  How often the "sysworkq" channel's heartbeat work item runs — it feeds
	  that channel, logs system-heap stats, and refreshes the CAD/AGC
	  statistics mirror (see meshtastic_watchdog.c). Must be well under
	  MESHTASTIC_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_MS (several beats per window) so a hang is
	  caught within roughly one timeout period, not two.

endif # MESHTASTIC_WATCHDOG
